{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 560,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you track the motion of individual stars in \nthe ultra-dense star cluster at the very center  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Milky Way you’ll see that they swing \nin sharp orbits around some vast but invisible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10440,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass—that’s the Sagittarius A* supermassive \nblack hole. These are perilous orbits,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16800,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and sometimes a star wanders just a little too \nclose to that lurking monster, leading to its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22600,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "utter destruction in the spectacular phenomenon \nknown as a tidal disruption event. Now we’ve never  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30080,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seen a TDE in the Milky Way, but we’ve seen them \nin other  galaxies—and we now know how to spot  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36800,
    "dDurationMs": 5848,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stellar destructions so extreme that they \nreveal properties of the black hole itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49750,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What happens when a star gets a little too close \nto a galaxy’s central supermassive black hole? Well as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it approaches, the gravitational force on the side \nof the star closer to the black hole gets stronger  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60840,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the pull on the far side. This gravitational \ndifferential results in a tidal force that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the star’s perspective, feels like it’s being \nstretched in the direction of the black hole and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71360,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compressed in the perpendicular direction. If the \nstar gets within a certain critical distance to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black hole—the tidal radius—the tidal force \nbecomes greater than the force of gravity binding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81680,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the star together. The star is smeared out in \na fluid-like, noodley mess in a process called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spaghettification. Some of this material gets \nejected outwards, while more spirals rapidly into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94000,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black hole, heating to incredible temperatures \nbefore disappearing into the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101520,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These tidal disruption events or TDEs are so rare \nthat we’ve never seen one in our own Sag A* black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107480,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole. But we’ve seen around 100 candidate TDEs in \ndistant galaxies. Even from Sag A*, and certainly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114880,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from other galaxies, a TDE appear to us as just a \nflash of light from the galactic center. But fear  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121920,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not! There is a way to get as close as we want to \none of these stellar trainwrecks: by recreating  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128720,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them in supercomputer simulations. By tuning those \nsimulations to our observations we're able to "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135240,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "study matter moving in the strange, warped \nspacetime around a black hole. And recently, we’ve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141200,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even predicted a type of extreme tidal disruption \nnever before seen, but we may see very soon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148200,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Throwing virtual stars at virtual black \nholes is actually enormously challenging,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we’ve only just pretty good at it. To \nunderstand how we do this and why it’s so hard,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let’s start with some of the gravitational \nphysics behind these tidal disruption events."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162600,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve been able to simulate motion \nunder gravity for some time now. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166840,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Apollo program did the orbital \nmechanics of its spacecraft on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171160,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“supercomputers” with the computing power \nof a hand-held calculator… from the 90s."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176160,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These simulations used Isaac Newton’s theory \nof gravity—calculating the gravitational force  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181080,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between all pairs of objects, seeing \nhow those forces change velocities  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185440,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and how those velocities change positions \nover small time steps, and then repeating  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190120,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over and over. The calculations are quick \nenough that modern computers can calculate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194240,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the interactions of millions of objects to \nsimulate entire galaxies, or even universes.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200760,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Newtonian gravity is only an \napproximation of how gravity really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "works—an approximation valid when gravity is \nweak. That’s fine for most of the universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209880,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even for mild tidal disruption events like \nthe one that may have torn apart a moon to form  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215360,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Saturn’s rings. But to properly describe a TDE \nnear a black hole we need the full theory—and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s Einstein’s general theory of relativity. \nRather than describing gravity as a force,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227200,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "general relativity, or GR describes the \nwarping of space and time by massive objects,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232600,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and explains gravity as the effect of \nthat warping on the motion of objects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237680,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although general relativity is incredibly \nwell-tested and certainly “correct”,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242120,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s also notoriously complicated. For one thing, \nthe nice symmetries that lead to clean circular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247080,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or elliptical orbits in Newtonian gravity no \nlonger apply in regions of space where GR becomes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252640,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "important. As a result, orbits around a black \nhole can be twisted and tangle over themselves,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "especially around rotating black holes. \nWithin a certain distance from the black hole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264680,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are no stable orbits whatsoever. This \ngravitational messiness has implications both  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271560,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a disrupted star and for the complexity of \nsimulating these disruptions on our computers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277760,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the star itself it means \nthat, within a certain distance,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280200,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all material must either fall into \nthe black hole or be flung back out.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284160,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can’t have nice ring systems of stellar \nmaterial in the region close to a black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290360,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the complexity of these orbits makes \nour simulations much more computationally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294040,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "challenging. In GR we describe the motion of \nobjects under gravity in terms of geodesics—paths  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "taken under pure gravity. To calculate the motion \nof an object in such a region, it’s not enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305840,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to calculate the force at every point—you \nneed to plot out the geodesics—in fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you need to build a map of the warped spacetime. \nIt gets much harder if you have to consider the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316680,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star’s own effect on that spacetime curvature, but \nfortunately for these TDEs the star’s mass is so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323720,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "piddling compared to the black hole that the star can \nbe neglected. But we have a different challenge.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330720,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once the star is disrupted, it’s no longer \na single object; it’s a smeared-out fluid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337920,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To simulate this we need relativistic \nhydrodynamics. We need to employ the equations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344560,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of fluid motion for material traveling at close \nto the speed of light in a warped spacetime. We  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350200,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to consider a variety of complex effects \nlike friction and the bath of intense radiation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356080,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produced by that superheater material which \ncan drive out the infalling material outwards. Oh,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361960,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we also need to simulate how that \nlight escapes the vicinity of the black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365920,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole to make its way to us. The same warped \nspacetime that destroyed the star also twists  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371000,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the paths of departing light rays in the \nphenomenon known as gravitational lensing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Until relatively recently, we’ve only been able \nto simulate piecemeal parts of this phenomenon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380720,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like the early time disruption of a star, \nor the formation of a disk. But these days,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "modern state of the art codes can follow a \ntidal disruption event from the incoming star,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390760,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all the way through its destruction to \nthe observable flashes of light we see."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395080,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cool, so what do these simulated TDEs actually look like? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400160,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are different types. The most \nimportant factor in determining what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happens is just how close the star gets to \nthe black hole—whether its orbit takes it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408800,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "within certain critical distances. The \nmost well-known such distance is the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event horizon—the surface of no escape. \nFor a non-rotating black hole this is at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417880,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Schwarzschild radius—around 3 km \nfor black hole with the Sun’s mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423480,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the star’s orbit would take it across that \nsurface, it is possible for it to drop directly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428480,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the black hole without any part of it \nescaping. This is called a direct capture event. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434360,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depending on the size of the black hole, it may \nbe tidally disrupted before entering—that’s the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439600,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "case if the tidal radius is larger than the event \nhorizon … true for any black hole smaller than a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444680,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "few million solar masses. Then the black \nhole slurps up the entire spaghettified  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450720,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stellar noodle. Larger than that and the star \nis still a star as it submerges. These direct  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458320,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "capture events are typically not produce much of \nan electromagnetic signature—the star just isn’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463800,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ravaged enough to shine brightly before vanishing forever. \nThese should also be quite rare due to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relatively small target of the black hole event \nhorizon, so we’ll leave these aside for now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475480,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the other end of the spectrum is the \npartial TDE. If the star passes close  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479560,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough to be significantly stretched but not \nenough to be disrupted—so outside the star’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tidal radius—then the star may shed significant \nmaterial from its outer layers, but enough remains  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489960,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intact that it remains a star—fusion continues \nin its core and it settles down upon escape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the real fireworks begin with the \nfull tidal disruption event—also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501880,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called the common TDE—because these \nare the ones we most commonly observe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506240,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to their brightness. This is \nwhen the star crosses the tidal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radius but its orbit doesn’t carry it \ndirectly through the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515040,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s take a look at a relativistic hydrodynamic \nsimulation of a common TDE to see what should  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happen. This is a simulation of a star three \ntimes the Sun’s mass passing within the tidal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526760,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radius of a 100,000 solar mass black hole. That’s \nwhere the tidal forces are now large enough that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they overcome the star’s self gravity. Over \nabout a day, the star completes its flyby,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539080,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it keeps expanding, producing a giant plume \nof plasma- the core of the star loses pressure,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545440,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and fusion cuts off. But that giant stream doesn’t \njust keep going, not all of it anyway. That tail  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552520,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "starts to fall back toward the black hole and then \nbegins to loop back in on itself. The material  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558120,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that hasn’t escaped ends up on an elliptical \norbit that can clearly be seen as the snake biting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564680,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its tail, and much of the material that didn’t \nescape finds stable orbits around the black hole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570520,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forming the start of an accretion disk. An important feature of this orbit is this violent choke-point at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576520,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "closest point of the orbit. This choke point is \ncalled a “nozzle shock” caused by the tremendous  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582440,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speeds that the stellar material attains. It’s \nat this point that friction and turbulence and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588280,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other forces in the stream of debris cause \npotentially observable flares of photons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594640,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A common TDE can be bright enough to outshine the \nentire surrounding galax. But it seems that that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599480,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe is capable of doing even better—even \nif we haven’t seen this yet. Simulations have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603640,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "revealed the possibility of something \nthat astrophysicists are calling an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607400,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extreme tidal disruption event—an eTDE. \nThis should happen when a star’s orbit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613360,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "takes it within the tidal radius of a black \nhole and very close to the event horizon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617560,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "say 2 to 3 Schwarzchild radii for a non-rotating \nblack hole. The result is a vastly more energetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624560,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "phenomenon than the common TDE. We don’t \nyet have a confirmed observation of an eTDE,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630520,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but recent simulations are helping us to \nunderstand exactly what to look for and how  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to look. Let’s take a look at the relativistic \nhydrodynamic simulation of this event. by Dr.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640480,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Taeho Ryu at the Max Planck Institute and \nJILA at CU Boulder. This was just published,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647480,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and is the first to follow the eTDE from a star \nwith realistic stellar structure to the hot  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654400,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mess that it gets shredded into. And by the way, the \nearlier simulations we saw are also by Dr. Ryu."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This time we’re throwing a 1 solar mass star \nat a million solar mass black hole, aiming to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "skim just outside the event horizon. It begins \nsomewhat similarly to the common TDE. However,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673160,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "within seconds we see direct capture of some \nof the star, with an emphasis on “some”. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679600,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rest of it is dragged out into a tail that \nwhips around the black hole multiple times,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684000,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leaving a spiral trail that was not \nseen at with the elliptical orbit of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fallback that made the common TDE.  Very soon an \nentirely new phase emerges – within tens of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695080,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minutes the windings of the tail merge together, \nleaving behind a circularized ring made up of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701320,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "last bits that narrowly escaped their demise. \nThese remaining bits now sit in an expanding,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "circular cloud. Though stable for quite some time, \nthe innermost portions of the ring nevertheless  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714720,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "begin to fall back toward the central black \nhole. As the inside of the ring fills in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720040,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "previously empty cavity, the black hole accretes \nthe material and a long lived accretion disk is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726680,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formed until eventually all of the surrounding \nmaterial is ultimately either consumed or ejected."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732800,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the matter gets accreted by the black hole, \nmuch of that gravitational potential energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736240,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is converted to radiation energy, making a \nbright burst of light. In the extreme TDE,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742520,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this “luminosity” increases very rapidly until it reaches \nwhat is called the Eddington luminosity. This is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749320,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a sort of upper limit on the radiative \noutput for a given black hole mass,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753840,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where the outward radiative force directly \nbalances the inward force of gravity;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758840,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any more radiation, and the material is blown outward \nby the radiative winds. Being at this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765520,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eddington limit puts the system in a state of \ntemporary equilibrium, with outward radiation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771080,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "balancing gravity. But after \na while, the outgoing radiation drops off and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776400,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole’s gravity dominates again, enabling \nit to finish gobbling up the stellar material."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These extreme TDEs should be rare because the \nstar has to make a very close approach. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it turns out that for larger supermassive black \nholes, these are the only tidal disruption events  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that can happen. That’s because, as the mass of a \nblack hole grows, both the event horizon and tidal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798960,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radius grow. But the event horizon grows faster, \nso that at a few tens of millions of solar masses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805720,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the tidal radius is very near or even inside the \nevent horizon. Approaching these masses, if the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811600,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star gets close enough to be disrupted at all, \nit’s also close enough to undergo an extreme TDE."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818440,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we now know what tidal disruption events \nshould look like if you’re actually close to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822880,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one. But the real value of these simulations \nis to predict what they look like from Earth  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827320,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so we can actually test our ideas. And \npotentially identify our first extreme TDE."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833120,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what do TDEs look like to us? Because they’re \ntoo far away to resolve the spatial details,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we really just see a point of light growing in \nthe center of a distant galaxy where we expect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844600,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the supermassive black hole to lie. That \npoint brightens as the star disrupts and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spreads out and then fades away as the stellar \nmaterial either falls into the black hole or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853840,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disperses. The timescale depends on the black \nhole mass. For a million solar mass black hole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859320,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the rise time is around a month and then, \ntypically, it will have faded away within  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863480,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a year. For a common TDE, most of that light \nis emitted at visible wavelengths. Flashes of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868570,
    "dDurationMs": 1950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light that grow over about a month and then fade \nover several months. We can also see radio flares  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870520,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as ejected material slams into the diffuse stuff \nin the surrounding galaxy, producing shockwaves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876640,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Extreme TDEs are expected to be quite a bit \nmore extreme, as one would hope. They should  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882600,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rise in brightness much more quickly. An eTDE \naround a million solar mass black hole should  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889000,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brighten over only a few hours instead of \na month and then maintain a more consistent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893840,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brightness than a common TDE for up to \na year before finally fading out. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is something that the new study revealed because \nthe simulations found this temporary equilibrium  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903360,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as outgoing radiation helps resist gravity. \nThose simulations also predicted super compact  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908680,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accretion with a temperature peaking at around a \nmillion Kelvin. This results in a broad spectrum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913640,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of light with most photons being X-rays, and \nis around 10 times brighter than a common TDE."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921000,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We find common TDEs in our visible-light \nsurveys of the sky. They don’t happen very often—every  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927560,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "several thousand years in a given galaxy, and \nmost commonly around smaller supermassive black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932640,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes. We’ve never seen one directly in the Milky \nWay, but there are observed remnants such as radio  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938140,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lobes in the galactic center that would indicate \nthat one occurred a few thousand years ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943600,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ETDEs should be rarer than common TDEs because \nthey require a closer approach tend to happen  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950200,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around larger black holes, which are rarer than \nsmaller ones. But Dr. Ryu and team estimate one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956560,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "per 15,000 years for a 20 million solar mass \nblack hole. To spot such a thing we need to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963600,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "monitor a lot of galaxies, which is something \nnew large field surveys can do—but we also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970240,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to do it at X-ray wavelengths, which is \nmuch harder than visible light wavelengths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately, we already have an instrument \nthat may be up to the task. eROSITA is an X-ray  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981560,
    "dDurationMs": 8251,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detector on Spektr-RG space observatory and it's \ncreated an X-ray survey of the entire sky—but unfortunately"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989811,
    "dDurationMs": 5389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "operation was suspended in 2022 before \ncovering the X-ray energies needed to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995200,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detect an eTDEs. But eROSITA will soon \nresume its operations and finish its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000760,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "survey in the next couple of years. And then \nour first eTDEs will hopefully shine out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this will be a rare chance to witness the \nmotion of matter in the region very close to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014160,
    "dDurationMs": 10080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a black hole event horizon. Comparing what \nwe see with our simulations will let us test  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024240,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our models of these violent events—how \nstars get ripped apart by black holes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030360,
    "dDurationMs": 7974,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and also our understanding of how gravity really \nworks in the most extreme regions of spacetime."
    } ]
  } ]
}
